WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.000
The Nightmare on Elm Street series is one of&nbsp;
horror's most enduring franchises. Throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.880
series, a whole lot has changed about this dream&nbsp;
demon. But some things stay the same in the town&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.920
of Springwood, Ohio. Here are some things that&nbsp;
happen in every Nightmare on Elm Street movie.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:20.640
Freddy leaves a trail of destruction wherever he&nbsp;
goes, but no matter how many people he slaughters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.120
he can never seem to kill that Final Girl.&nbsp;
The main hero of the Nightmare on Elm Street&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.840
movies always gets away. Even when it looked&nbsp;
like he killed Nancy in the original film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.640
she showed up in Dream Warriors very much alive.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.880
Granted, the Final Girl always has a&nbsp;
tough time. She barely out-maneuvers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.240
Freddy throughout the story while&nbsp;
he hacks and slashes her friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.280
And in the end, just when it seems like Freddy&nbsp;
is about to add the Final Girl to his score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:50.000
she pulls the rug out from under our big burned&nbsp;
boy, and Freddy’s the one who ends up dead.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.600
Of course, sometimes, the Final Girl in one movie&nbsp;
becomes a "regular girl" in a subsequent movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.560
At that point, all bets are off, and Freddy&nbsp;
may very well shuffle her off this mortal coil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.680
And we should also point out that not all Final&nbsp;
Girls are actually girls. In Freddy's Revenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.080
that role falls to Jesse Walsh, who's&nbsp;
technically a Final Boy. Granted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.280
his fate is left somewhat ambiguous at the&nbsp;
end of the film, but we never see him die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.480
and if Nancy survived her bizarro dream&nbsp;
ending, we're sure Jesse is probably fine.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.280
Freddy always attacks his victims in&nbsp;
their dreams. His victims occasionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.080
figure out how to pull him into&nbsp;
the real world, but Freddy begins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.880
stalking potential human pincushions in the&nbsp;
astral plane before it gets to that point.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.120
That's also one of the main reasons why&nbsp;
Freddy makes such an unsettling figure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.000
Everyone has to sleep, and if Freddy attacks&nbsp;
in dreams where he has absolute power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.080
then Freddy can kill anyone at&nbsp;
any time, any way he wants to.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.280
Ergo, every Nightmare on Elm Street film&nbsp;
contains scenes of characters drinking coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.600
munching down caffeine pills, blasting&nbsp;
loud music directly into their earholes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.080
and engaging in various other activities&nbsp;
designed to avoid or delay sleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.880
It's a futile effort — but the cannon fodder&nbsp;
kids in Nightmare movies all have to try.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.760
Oddly enough, the Freddy movies depict&nbsp;
relationships between teenagers and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:14.960
adult guardians reasonably accurately. As anyone&nbsp;
who remembers being a teenager knows, whenever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.200
teens find themselves trapped in life-or-death&nbsp;
battles against demonic forces, they immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.840
learn that parents, teachers, and professional&nbsp;
mental health experts are all completely useless.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.200
Making matters worse, grownups typically insist&nbsp;
the kids are simply stressed out and need a nap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.800
These so-called "responsible" people always claim&nbsp;
the unholy menace doesn't exist when, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.600
some of them personally helped burn him alive&nbsp;
after the judge let him off on a technicality.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.040
Nancy's dad provides an exception&nbsp;
that proves the rule in Nightmare 3:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.280
Dream Warriors when he tries to come through for&nbsp;
the gang at the Westin Hills Psychiatric Center.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.200
Even then, he was a major skeptic in&nbsp;
the first film, and he only decides to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.400
be useful after he develops alcoholism due to&nbsp;
Freddy killing Nancy's mom. In the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.720
if a grownup happens to be chronically&nbsp;
depressed and constantly drunk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.680
that grownup will be about as effective against&nbsp;
supernatural malevolence as a bag of potatoes.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.680
For slasher fans, there's a sadistic glee in&nbsp;
witnessing homicidal mayhem that would traumatize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.720
us all into a coma if we saw it happen in real&nbsp;
life. This is extra true for Nightmare devotees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.520
because Freddy kills any way he feels like&nbsp;
killing, even if it defies the laws of physics.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:25.120
Johnny Depp made his big-screen debut as&nbsp;
Nancy's well-meaning boyfriend — in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:31.040
original 1984 movie. Freddy pulls Glen into&nbsp;
what may be Hell, then shoots his blood back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.240
up through his mattress, soaking the bedroom&nbsp;
ceiling in a geyser of crimson Glen juice.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:41.040
In later films, Freddy's homicidal tendencies&nbsp;
take a turn for the goofy and gimmicky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.040
There's a character who likes comic books&nbsp;
in Nightmare 5: The Dream Child, so Freddy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:48.960
physically sucks him into a comic book and&nbsp;
cuts him up while he's two-dimensional.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.480
There's a character who smokes weed in&nbsp;
Freddy's Dead, so Freddy turns into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.720
psychedelic hallucination and kills him that&nbsp;
way. Sometimes he takes himself seriously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.200
but other times, Freddy is basically&nbsp;
Willy Wonka, except a troupe of Oompa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.520
Loompas don't appear to sing a judgmental&nbsp;
song after he destroys a young human life.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.640
One of the most disturbing things&nbsp;
about Freddy is how he can morph and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:15.040
manipulate his body whenever he feels&nbsp;
like it. It's a staple of the series.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.280
For example, Freddy makes his arms grow&nbsp;
absurdly long right before he indulges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.200
in his first onscreen taste of slaughter.&nbsp;
In the second film, he explodes out of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.040
young man's midsection, then heads across&nbsp;
the room to stab another hapless teen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:32.480
But Freddy doesn't get genuinely hyped about&nbsp;
shapeshifting until Nightmare 3: Dream Warriors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:38.240
He morphs his fingers into syringes to murder&nbsp;
a recovering addict, becomes a big ol' worm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.200
and he turns himself into a television set before&nbsp;
electrocuting a mentally ill girl to death.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.080
Even in New Nightmare, Freddy puts Gene&nbsp;
Simmons to shame with his ever-growing tongue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.240
Stabbing and slashing might be Freddy's&nbsp;
go-to choice for a quick and easy notch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.560
on his belt. But when it comes time to kill&nbsp;
someone in the weirdest manner possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:02.080
this slasher never hesitates to change himself&nbsp;
into some of the most ridiculous stuff imaginable.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.320
One of the creepiest things about Freddy&nbsp;
Krueger is that he's got his own theme song.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.640
And the frightening tune makes&nbsp;
an appearance in every film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.200
Most of the time, it's a small group of little&nbsp;
kids singing it to keep time while they skip rope.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.160
Since Freddy’s methods vary to wild extremes&nbsp;
depending on the characteristics of his victim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:26.880
Freddy doesn't have a signature catchphrase&nbsp;
because no single pun or one-liner would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:31.840
effective for the majority of his murdering. But&nbsp;
the wholesome image of little kids skipping rope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:37.760
serves the same purpose, both on a visual and&nbsp;
sonic level. As we know from many other films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:42.480
little kids in horror movies are the&nbsp;
creepiest things ever. All they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:47.920
to do is walk into a shot, sing a spooky song,&nbsp;
and it'll make you pee your pants on the spot.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:52.800
There aren't a ton of constants that stick&nbsp;
throughout Nightmare movies. But if you're a total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.200
psycho who genuinely wants to see Freddy Krueger&nbsp;
come out on top, then the experience of watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:03.920
these films must make for a profoundly frustrating&nbsp;
experience. Why? Because Freddy always loses.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:08.320
Freddy walks away with his fair share of&nbsp;
consolation prizes, no doubt. After his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:12.480
inevitable vanquishing, he might jump out of&nbsp;
seemingly nowhere and snatch up someone's mom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:16.640
he might put his razor hand through a young&nbsp;
woman's torso, or he might wink at the camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.600
as Jason Voorhees carries his severed head&nbsp;
off-screen. He wins these sneaky, after-the-fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.400
victories that mainly serve to leave the door&nbsp;
open for a possible sequel. But that's only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:31.360
after he comes up short in whatever his primary&nbsp;
goal happened to be during the particular story.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:36.480
He can't win because one or more of his&nbsp;
prospective victims always manages to outsmart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:41.520
and outmatch him. But if Freddy Krueger's purpose&nbsp;
is to kill a handful of people, face defeat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.000
then return in a couple of years when studio&nbsp;
executives decide it’s time for another movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.600
maybe the concepts of winning and&nbsp;
losing don't concern him that much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:53.440
Perhaps Freddy Krueger is just a guy&nbsp;
with a job to do, like the rest of us.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:59.840
The slasher subgenre frequently finds itself&nbsp;
accused of sameness and redundancy — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:05.760
rightly so. Plenty of slasher movies are exactly&nbsp;
the same. An invincible monster is introduced, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:10.160
one by one, it cuts down a roster of attractive&nbsp;
20-something actors playing high schoolers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:15.680
A sole-surviving protagonist vanquishes the&nbsp;
adversary and escapes, and then the movie ends.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.240
However, filmmakers started taking risks&nbsp;
with Freddy as early as the first sequel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:25.120
A Nightmare on Elm Street 2: Freddy's Revenge&nbsp;
repurposes Robert Englund's signature villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:29.920
into a metaphor for repressed homosexuality. The&nbsp;
third movie, Dream Warriors, takes place in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:34.960
mental asylum for troubled youths. At one point,&nbsp;
Freddy turns into a giant worm version of himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:40.160
to eat a young Patricia Arquette. It's not as bold&nbsp;
as Freddy's Revenge, but it's definitely shocking.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:44.480
The first three Nightmare films are all totally&nbsp;
distinct from each other. And the following three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.680
movies are all too ridiculous to be predictable.&nbsp;
Freddy turns his victims into a supernatural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:55.680
pizza in Nightmare on Elm Street 4: The Dream&nbsp;
Master. That's not even scary. It's just bonkers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.960
Things get especially wild with New Nightmare,&nbsp;
where director Wes Craven turns the metatextual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:07.531
irony up to 11 by having Krueger be an actual&nbsp;
demon that exists outside the Nightmare movies.

00:08:07.531 --> 00:08:13.226
"Love ya babe! We’ll do lunch”

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:18.400
In other words, Freddy Krueger doesn't repeat&nbsp;
himself. His movies might not all be great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:20.720
but at least he's never boring.

